    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nNot an Optical Illusion: Four Differently Sized Bentley Blowers Come Together for the First Time\n14 photos\nPhoto: Bentley\nI’m pretty sure every single day something comes along to trick your eyes and brains. The images we have here, showing the historically important Bentley Blower in no less than four different sizes, are not that, as this is not an optical illusion, but an actual gathering of Blowers inspired by a piece of engineering from long ago.\nThere’s a big chance most of you now reading this know what the Blower is all about. Officially titled the 1929 Bentley 4½-liter, the supercharged machine was one of four Bentleys created for racing purposes on the tracks of the Old Continent, including at the 24 Hours of Le Mans in 1930.\nTeam Car No. 2, with registration number UU 5872 was the source of inspiration for the creation of its smaller siblings you can see in the photos attached to this text. One of them is the Bentley Blower Jnr road legal scale car that was first announced three years ago by Hedley Studios (formerly known as The Little Car Company), and the other two much smaller replicas put together by Amalgam Collection.\nWe’ve discussed the Jnr several times since it was first announced, the last time in spring of last year, when Bentley and Hedley announced the start of production. Now, more than a year after that time, the British companies took the opportunity to let us all know that the initial run of 25 scale Blowers are ready to be delivered to their owners.\nPhoto: Bentley This vehicle is an 85 percent scale version of the original Blower, powered by a fully electric powertrain that delivers 31 hp and is capable of carrying the slammer car to a top speed of 50 mph (80 kph). The maximum range the car can travel on a single charge is 75 miles (120 km).\nThe above numbers are not set in stone, as they vary depending on region and local regulations. Over in Europe, for instance, the top speed is limited to 45 mph (72 kph), while over in the U.S. it is capped at 25 mph (40 kph). Naturally, there are no restrictions when it comes to range,\nThe 25 Jnrs that have already been made are part of a limited run of 349 that will ever be produced, with the very first one already delivered to Bentley’s headquarters in Crewe. Each of these scale cars is said to cost around $120,000 – which is pennies, if you consider the fact that the original it is based on is valued at around $34 million.\nThe other two replicas of the Blower, made by Amalgam Collection, are not as impressive as the Jnr, of course, and that’s owed to the fact that they are not only smaller, but also far less mechanically complex.\nPhoto: Bentley There are two Amalgam variants available, a 1:18 one and a 1:8 variant that has been around for six years already. The former is the newest addition to the range, as is the race-weathered version that accompanies them.\nDespite their size, the tiny Blowers are not to be taken lightly. Bentley describes one of the two toys (Amalgam might resent that term, but that is what they are, if you really think about it), the 1:18 version, as the most technically accomplished model Amalgam has ever made.\nWhat these scale models lack in terms of, well, everything when compared to the Jnr, they make up in exclusivity. Owners of the Blower Continuation Series cars can have either one of the two scale replicas configured and crafted in such a way as to mirror the full-size cars.\nDespite their very small size, the two scale Blowers are not exactly cheap to own. The 1:18 version sells at the time of writing for $1,595, while the larger 1:8 model carries a sticker that reads $26,995.\nPhoto: Bentley When it comes to the race-weathered version of its 1:8 scale model, Amalgam will only make 30 of them, and each will be hand-painted in such a way that they recreate as faithfully as possible the dirt, patina, and battle scars a car usually gains in the heat of the race.\nMore specifically, the design of these 30 units mirrors the look of Team Car No.2 after its 1930 presence at the 24 Hours of Le Mans. That includes, believe it or not, the bent rear fenders that resulted from tire failures.\nDespite its intentional battle scars, this version of the most expensive Bentley in Amalgam’s collection is selling for at least $27,875 at the time of writing.\nAs for the car that inspired the three differently sized replicas, Team Car No. 2 is no longer actively racing, but that doesn’t mean it’s completely idle in a garage somewhere. Now part of the Bentley Heritage Collection, it makes appearances at various events across the world, including the ones that involve actual driving.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBentley Bentley Blower Bentley Blower Jnr amalgam\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Rare Chevy Camaro ZL1 Looks Like a GTA VI Prop, Yours If You Really Want It",
